#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3003d2c6-3b38-5e95-b719-5b9a0d5c9642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283000} {:answer/id #uuid "02ed77c0-f3e4-541a-8b4b-30cb5e3a1ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283001} {:answer/id #uuid "dab393c9-b76c-5660-bb79-30c2d41d6439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283002} {:answer/id #uuid "e84b7c88-92b9-57ba-a71c-0fc867044a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283003} {:answer/id #uuid "58eefba0-9a07-54df-9842-45ae83596040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02ed77c0-f3e4-541a-8b4b-30cb5e3a1ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283001} :answer-field/id #uuid "746eb990-d795-58ac-b486-d547ad6ba17a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9233943-9639-5785-9a46-de416a7119f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-15641" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=9-\\frac{2}{3}x$ and the point $P(3,7)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(7).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(3,7).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =9-\\frac{2}{3}x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(3,7)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(7,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(7) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1aaed71-57db-59c5-ab3c-c1667fd06692" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283007} {:answer/id #uuid "73cb4b26-e2b5-5bba-b128-7e87e156618d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283008} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1a7af8-21b1-5e1a-8c0c-e73e0647e4a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283009} {:answer/id #uuid "b96377cf-d0db-5d3a-b091-b65ef9f6fa37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283010} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc7c712-f332-5322-aa66-499961a595aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fc7c712-f332-5322-aa66-499961a595aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283011} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f7a5989-e6d1-5515-b407-8df842245c56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d6fc4e8-faa4-5439-8ad8-f1b03b3d2be1" :question/math-academy-id "q-15646" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+3}{5}$ and the point $P(2,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(2,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+3}{5} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(2,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d35ad3ef-28f1-557d-bbec-6ef6c4696fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d35ad3ef-28f1-557d-bbec-6ef6c4696fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283014} :answer-field/id #uuid "071430b0-ac1c-5c5d-bfb0-7aacd0d96541" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec34106c-1275-5721-882b-cb32e2aa950e" :question/math-academy-id "q-342589" :question/problem "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $-1$ | $6$ |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(-1)=-2$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0eec1c4-c10a-532c-a5fa-9e263ee5696a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0eec1c4-c10a-532c-a5fa-9e263ee5696a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283017} :answer-field/id #uuid "894baeef-f0ad-5392-955d-aa043b1917b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "203d422f-9b1d-5a14-a3ec-68b4a7ed16ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-342609" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $0\\le x<2,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(-1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(-1)=x.$ For $x\\ne 2,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(-1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2}\\cdot (x-2) & =-1\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x-2)} & =-x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $0\\le x<2,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(-1)=1.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{1}^{2}-4(1)}{(1-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41fa0ec3-6439-5312-96af-0e8246b6cfae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283020} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6b3d19-9784-5eb5-8652-e71e9e717af0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283021} {:answer/id #uuid "9845bc1d-ed36-504a-bbab-69efb069096b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283022} {:answer/id #uuid "deafd93e-778e-58ec-ad94-e8291c6203d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283023} {:answer/id #uuid "b4cab2b6-9199-5233-996b-ab564430dc11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9845bc1d-ed36-504a-bbab-69efb069096b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283022} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd804082-47b5-53ff-bc11-5feb8f316449" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8c7f142-6d6f-5e69-8355-8be41cf102c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-75987" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(4).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(4)=x.$ For $x\\ne -1,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(4) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{x+1} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1) & =4\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{\\cancel{(x+1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x+1)} & =4x+4 \\\\ 2x+1 & =4x+4 \\\\ 2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(4)=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
